   "id": "b2-44",
   "name": "Harmful practices: early marriage and female genital mutilation"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Some traditional practices harm the health and rights of girls.\nEarly (child) marriage is the marriage of a child under 18. International agreements signed by Cameroon and its Penal Code protect children from forced marriage.\nCauses: poverty (bride price), tradition, wanting to “protect” girls, low value placed on girls' education.\nConsequences: the girl leaves school; early pregnancy is dangerous for a young body (obstetric fistula, death in childbirth); violence; poverty continues.\nFemale genital mutilation (FGM) is cutting or removing part of a girl's external genital organs for non-medical reasons. It is forbidden by law in Cameroon.\nConsequences: severe pain and bleeding, infections (including HIV from shared blades), problems in passing urine, problems in childbirth, and lasting emotional harm.\nGirls at risk can tell a teacher, health worker or social worker.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "x": "a girl of 14 is to be married"
  },
  "problem": "Give two consequences for her health and education if {x}.",
  "steps": [
   "She will probably stop going to school.",
   "Pregnancy in a young body is dangerous: it can cause fistula or death in childbirth."
  ],
  "answer": "She leaves school, and early pregnancy endangers her life.",
  "checks": []
 },
